From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 09 Oct 2026 20:06:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] MPEG: hold ring packets until their access units are taken

A movie player that tells "the movie is over" by an entirely free ring
buffer (end-of-file flag set and sceMpegRingbufferAvailableSize equal to the
ring size) was told so as soon as the demultiplexer had read the last pack,
while the last audio frames were still queued here and had never been
fetched. The game then left its frame loop with its two-deep decoded-frame
ring full; its audio/video sync held the last picture back because the audio
clock (the last audio block it had fetched) could not reach the picture's
time, and its drain loop, which does not present frames, waited for a free
slot forever. Whether it hung depended on the guest's speed
(<prefix>_CPU_NS_PER_INSN 0, 1 and 4 hung, 8 did not).

A pack now stays counted as filled until every unit of a registered stream
that was complete when it was read has been fetched with sceMpegGetAvcAu or
sceMpegGetAtracAu. <prefix>_MPEG_EAGER_RELEASE=1 restores the old behaviour.

Also: bring-up tooling (switch trace, thread backtraces, per-call return
values, post-import hook).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

